From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:34:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(worker): keep embedding and output-head weights in the
 GB10 display carve-out

DGX Spark firmware reserves about 2 GiB at the top of physical memory for
display scanout. Ordinary allocations never use it, and a text console takes
only its framebuffer (8 MiB at 1920x1080). With
SPARK3_DISPLAY_CARVEOUT_WEIGHTS=1 the worker moves the vocabulary-sized
embedding and output-head weights into a DRM dumb buffer from that carve-out
(exported as a dma-buf, imported with cuImportExternalMemory) right after
loading, before kernel plans or graphs capture them, and returns their
ordinary memory for the KV cache.

Measured on GB10, the imported mapping streams at full speed but gives no
reuse: sequential reads run at 235 GB/s and a 6-row GEMV over the 43K-row
head runs 10% faster than from ordinary memory, while random 576-byte reads
run at 7 GB/s against 221 GB/s and large-M GEMMs run 2.3x slower. Holding the
KV cache there cost 8% at eight streams and in prefill, so only these
read-once tables move. The worker refuses to start if the carve-out cannot
hold them.
